| [killzone](../projects/killzone/00-overview.md) | building | **phase 1 complete 2026-09-21, first session** — a top-down shooter with a Diablo bird view: a man with a rifle on a 60 m square of grass, mouse aim independent of movement, and forward/backward/strafe/jump/crouch/fire all animated. Nine animation clips built from the seven the Sketchfab model shipped — backpedal plays the legs in reverse against a forward torso, strafe yaws the hips 68°, the jump comes from pushing a stand-to-squat blend past both ends (`tools/build_character.py`). Three animation faults were caught only by rendering contact sheets and looking; none raised an error. Hitscan firing at ~9 rounds/sec with pooled tracers and a muzzle flash on the rifle bone; the recoil plays on the upper body only so he keeps running while firing. Verified by a self-driving screenshot harness (`tools/capture.tscn`). **Shipped facing backward and was fixed the same day** — the build script's 180° yaw had always been a no-op because Blender's glTF importer leaves objects in quaternion rotation mode, where `rotation_euler` is silently ignored while `scale` on the next line works. It passed review because the check was numeric (facing · aim = 1.0) and measured the CharacterBody3D, which was right; the model inside it was turned around. There are now visual checks, `capture.tscn -- facing` and `-- clips`. **2026-09-22: the recoil rebuilt** — it was keyed neutral→kick→settle→neutral over 0.30 s while the rifle fires every 0.11 s and each shot seeks back to frame zero, so it only ever played its first third and snapped through neutral on every shot; it now opens on the kick and decays, with the rotation moved to the chest so the grip survives and the neck counter-pitched so the head does not whip. Two defects found then and **left unfixed as out of scope**: shots are aimed at a plane 1.0 m up while the muzzle is at 1.92 m, so aiming 8 m out buries the round at 16.2 m; and the model imports **2.73 m tall** against a 1.8 m collider. **Nothing to shoot at, no sound, no objective** — all deliberate; the next conversation is what the game *is*, not what to build next. Private repo at github.com/juslangit/killzone since 2026-09-23 | 2026-09-21 | 2026-09-21 | `projects/game/killzone/` |
| [gerak](../projects/gerak/00-overview.md) | in use | **2026-09-23, fifth session: a clip cut to 20 frames stayed 45.** Both exporters only ever extended a track to reach the end of the clip, never shortened one — so limiting an animation's length changed what gerak played and nothing about what it wrote, and reopening showed a 20-frame timeline whose keys still ran to 45 (D-030). The key at the new end is sampled, not copied from the last key before it, and nothing is deleted: the row says `25 after the end` and lengthening brings them back. **Fourth session: the push that wrote nothing.** He edited keyframes, pressed Save, pressed Update the game, and the game did not change — the log said `pushed nothing`. Save cleared the very flag the push reads, so the two buttons fought (D-026); saved edits were never read back when a character was reopened, so work sitting safely in the clips folder looked lost (D-027); and restoring a reference picture on every animation switch marked the clip edited, which is why a few edits saved fourteen clips (D-028). Four of his edits — fb_fall, fb_idle, fb_keeper_catch, fb_keeper_dive — come back on open and are queued for the game. **Third session: used or unused, and deleting.** Each animation now says beside its keys whether the game actually asks for it, from one search of the game's own files for the name in quotes — on red-card's footballer that is 36 of 53 used and 17 not, the tennis and volleyball moves that came with the shared rig (D-024). Delete on one or more ticked animations shows the exact lines that name them before it goes ahead (D-025). Two bugs found in the same day's code: a 40-hit cap that made later names read as unused, and openAnim clearing the undo stack so deleting the open animation could not be undone. **Second session: a character's whole set of animations.** Opening a model used to prompt for one of the animations in the file and put the rest out of reach — Red Card's footballer has 53 and referee-for-fun's athletes have 12, so all but one were unreachable. Now the Animations tab lists them all, any one is editable, edits survive switching, and undo covers the lot (D-018). **Merge two…** keeps whichever motion is chosen and deletes the other name, after searching the game's own scripts for it and showing every line that says it — seven in `scripts/footballer.gd` the first time it ran (D-020). **Update the game** writes the edited animations straight back into the game's `.glb` by editing its glTF JSON in place, not through Blender: only what was edited is rewritten, the mesh, skin, materials and untouched animations come out byte for byte, orphans are collected so the file never grows, keys are thinned (1,509 → 314 on one athlete), and a copy goes to `~/Documents/gerak/backups` first (D-019, D-021 — the one exception to D-008). 66 new checks; 309 total. **2026-09-23: used for real work at last (M7)** — Luqman re-keyed the run of Red Card's footballer by hand inside bengkel, and every running animation in that game is now his clip; the first work gerak has done that left the tool. **2026-09-22: reference pictures.** A search panel over Wikimedia Commons with Muybridge's frame-by-frame motion studies first — pin one over the viewport, fade it, and Follow steps it with the timeline proportionally (frame 24 of 48 lands on reference frame 6 of 12); Flip plays it on its own. Saved with the clip, picture copied to disk, served by gerak's own server so the page never talks to Commons. 205 checks. **2026-09-22: joined bengkel.** Its library now has a row of chips saying where each model came from, boneka pinned first (213 rigged models one click away); it can send an animated model back to boneka; and it answers bengkel's menu bar. All behind a check for bengkel, so gerak alone is unchanged. built 2026-09-21 — pose by hand, FK/IK with pinning, template rigging through Blender, exports to .glb/.fbx/.blend/.mp4. Wrapped the same day as a real Mac app in /Applications: own window, menu bar, drag and drop, opens from Finder, starts and stops its own server. Six engine suites plus 32 app checks, all green. **Used by hand for real work on 2026-09-23** (M7 done) | 2026-09-21 | 2026-09-23 | `projects/3d/bengkel/gerak/` |
| [notula](../projects/notula/00-overview.md) | testing | v1 built 2026-09-21 — records room + call, local Whisper, Claude writes the notes; not yet used on a real meeting | 2026-09-21 | 2026-09-21 | `projects/ai/notula/` |
| [siti-sihat](../projects/siti-sihat/00-overview.md) | testing | v1 built 2026-09-21 in one session — a six-feature healthy-lifestyle web app for the phone: water reminder, cut-sugar tracker, three guided routines (jawline, inner thigh, core) sharing one player with spoken cues and countdown beeps, and a menstrual tracker that predicts from the user's own logged history rather than a fixed 28 days. Plain HTML, CSS and ES modules — no framework, no build step, no dependencies — installable as a PWA and working offline. Everything stays in `localStorage` on the device (D-002), and each routine states plainly what it cannot do: no thigh gap from adductor work, no spot reduction from crunches (D-003). 23 checks green through a self-driving headless-Chrome harness, which caught nothing that three screenshots did not — the back arrow on the home screen, icons reading as a padlock and two drinking glasses, and a chart rendering as seven empty boxes. **Published 2026-09-22**: public repo at github.com/juslangit/siti-sihat, live at **https://siti-sihat.pages.dev** (Cloudflare Pages, primary — Netlify refused every deploy with "Account credit usage exceeded") and mirrored at https://juslangit.github.io/siti-sihat/ — moved off the LAN route because the Mac's address changed overnight and plain HTTP is not a secure context, so iOS would not have registered the service worker. Verified live: secure context, worker registered at the right scope, manifest and icons served, no console errors. **Never opened on a real phone** — that is M8 and the only thing that matters next | 2026-09-21 | 2026-09-22 | `projects/siti-sihat/` |
| [bengkel](../projects/bengkel/00-overview.md) | testing | **2026-09-23: an assistant in every tool (M15).** A floating panel, injected into every page bengkel hosts, that asks the `claude` command already on the Mac — no API key, nothing billed per question. It may only call actions each tool declares (26 to start: gerak 10, jaring 4, hantar 4, boneka 3, pasar 2, kulit 2, periksa 1), which is what makes every change undoable: it photographs the tool first, twenty deep, and gerak lends its own history rather than keeping a second. Anything reaching outside a tool — updating a game, shipping, building, remeshing — always asks first and is deliberately left off the undo stack. First real turn: "move the playhead to frame 12", done and undone. 59 checks added. **2026-09-23: used for real work (M6)** — Luqman re-keyed Red Card's run in gerak on the rail and it went into the game the same day; one tool of the seven, not the rail, so M7 and M14 stay open. built 2026-09-22 and turned into a **full 3D asset pipeline** the same day. One macOS window with a rail in the order the work happens — **find it, make it, clean it up, dress it, check it, move it, ship it** — holding seven tools: **pasar** (Sketchfab, Poly Haven and Texturelabs in one search, licence on every card before the download), **boneka**, **jaring** (new remesher: one slider asking how wide a quad should be in centimetres, then weld, Quadriflow, UVs, a baked normal map and LODs through headless Blender — a paladin went 12,332 → 2,632 triangles in 3s, same size and still recognisably a paladin), **kulit** (a colour and a material per part, with detail, normal and roughness maps derived from one photograph), **periksa** (size, origin, weight and UVs read straight out of the .glb in milliseconds, judged against what the model is for), **gerak**, and **hantar** (into one of the 12 game projects, under that project's own naming, standing on the floor, .glb for Godot or .fbx at 100 units/m for Unreal). Verified as a loop, not just tool by tool: periksa flagged a model's origin, hantar fixed it, periksa passed it. Everything the tools share lives in `common/`, so an eighth is a page and ~100 lines. ~145 checks green, boneka's 801 and gerak's suites unbroken. **First real work done on 2026-09-23** (M6 done; M7 and M14 open — one tool, not the rail) | 2026-09-22 | 2026-09-23 | `projects/3d/bengkel/` |
| [kasih-alza](../projects/kasih-alza/00-overview.md) | live | **live 2026-09-23** at **https://kasih-alza.pages.dev** — two-house homestay site for Bachang, Melaka: bilingual EN/BM, both video tours, 20 room photos cut out of the WhatsApp posters, a live availability calendar on Supabase and an owner page to block dates and edit prices. **2026-09-23: the owner signs in on the site itself** — a quiet **Admin** button in the header opens a sign-in box over the page, and a signed-in owner taps the customer's own calendar to mark dates occupied or free (D-011, M13). A new `tools/check.mjs` drives headless Chrome through the whole route against the real Supabase project, 36 checks, proving the database refuses the write rather than the page hiding a button; it deletes its own test booking even when a check fails. Sign-in is by **username** rather than email since the same day (D-012), and the write policies were found granting everything to the whole `authenticated` role — with the anon key published and signup open, a stranger could have signed themselves up and read every guest note; they now name the two admin user ids (D-013, M14). 38 checks. Netlify is credit-blocked; after a few hours on a public GitHub Pages mirror it moved to Cloudflare Pages by direct upload, so the source repo stays private with no public copy (D-008, D-010). Open: the exact map pin, and photos at camera resolution rather than ~315px poster crops | 2026-09-22 | 2026-09-23 | `projects/web/kasih-alza/` |
