# Methods — how this project is worked on

## Setup from scratch
```bash
git clone https://github.com/juslangit/Portfolio.git ~/Desktop/project/web/portfolio
cd ~/Desktop/project/web/portfolio
open index.html          # that's it — no install, no server
```

## Daily commands
| Purpose | Command |
|---|---|
| Preview | `open index.html` |
| Deploy | `./tools/deploy.sh` — uploads to Cloudflare Pages (`luqmanhakeem`). `git push` only saves to the private repo; it publishes nothing |
| Verify live | `curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" https://luqmanhakeem.pages.dev/` |
| Verify records are locked | `curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" https://luqmanhakeem.pages.dev/records/` must print `302` |

## Folder structure
```
portfolio/
├── index.html    # the entire site — markup, styles and scripts
├── README.md
└── .gitignore
```

## Conventions
### Code
- One file. If it ever needs a second, that is a decision worth recording first.
- No dependencies beyond the Three.js CDN tag and the Google Fonts link.
- Plain ES5-style JS (`var`, `function`) — matches the no-build-step spirit and needs no transpiling.
- The `<script>` is divided into numbered, commented sections (1 cursor, 2 magnetic,
  3 tilt, 4 reveal, 5 nav, 6 hero 3D, 7 confetti, 8 easter egg). Keep that numbering.

### Git
- Branch: `feat/<short-description>`
- Commit: imperative, lowercase
- Pushing `main` publishes immediately — check the page renders before pushing

## Patterns in use

### Verify by rendering, not by reading
**When to use:** before every push.
**Why:** reading the code missed both real bugs found on 2026-09-12.
```bash
CHROME="/Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome"
# screenshot + console errors
"$CHROME" --headless --disable-gpu --virtual-time-budget=8000 \
  --window-size=1440,1400 --screenshot=/tmp/shot.png \
  --enable-logging=stderr --v=0 file://$PWD/index.html 2>&1 | grep -i CONSOLE
# syntax-check the inline script
python3 -c "import re;s=open('index.html').read();open('/tmp/i.js','w').write(re.findall(r'<script>(.*?)</script>',s,re.S)[-1])"
node --check /tmp/i.js
```

### Testing phone width
**Gotcha:** headless Chrome clamps its viewport to a 500px minimum, so
`--window-size=390,...` renders at 500 and merely *crops* the screenshot — which
looks exactly like a horizontal-overflow bug. Load the page in a 390px-wide
`<iframe>` from a wrapper page instead, and screenshot that.

### Every claim must trace to an artifact
**When to use:** any time a number or adjective goes on the page.
Two invented stats ("100+ students taught", "6+ years") and one overstatement
("a decade of visual craft") were caught and replaced before publishing, with
numbers that come from real files: 64,770 lots from the peta-lot-tanah README,
5 sports from referee-for-fun, 0 npm dependencies from claude-chat.
He will be asked about these in an interview.

## A card with two destinations

Most work cards are one big `<a>`. A card that also links its project record
cannot be, because an anchor may not contain another anchor — those cards are a
`<div class="work …">` with a `.foots` row holding both links (the game or repo,
and **Read the record →**). Keep the `work` class when converting: the hover lift
and the reveal animation are attached to it, not to the tag.

Record links point at https://luqman-docs.netlify.app/<project>/ — see
`global/conventions.md` → "Documentation is a website".

## Portfolio sync — when a project changes

> Luqman's standing rule, set 2026-09-12: **whenever a project is created or
> updated, or anything else relevant changes, the portfolio gets updated too.**

A `PostToolUse` hook (`~/.claude/hooks/portfolio-sync-reminder.sh`, wired in
`~/.claude/settings.json`) fires whenever `_index/projects.md` is written, which
is exactly when a project is created or changes status. It injects this checklist
back into the session. The hook covers the mechanical trigger; the cases below
that it cannot detect are on you to catch.

### The checklist
| What changed | What to check in `index.html` |
|---|---|
| A project was added | Bump the **Shipped projects** stat. Decide whether it earns a featured card — and if so, whether it displaces a weaker one. |
| A project changed status | The card's tag: `Public repo` / `In testing` / `Prototype` / `Private repo`. |
| A repo went public | Turn the card into an `<a>` with the repo URL, and swap the tag. |
| A project shipped something measurable | Consider whether it beats an existing stat. Sourced numbers only. |
| A new tool, language or service was adopted | The **Toolkit** chips in the dark About card (Code / AI & Data / 3D & Engines / Design & Make). |
| A project was archived or abandoned | Remove or demote its card; correct the Shipped projects count. |
| A game or 3D piece was finished | The 3D & games section, or the earlier-work tiles. |
| Luqman's role or education changed | The timeline in the About card. |

### Rules that apply to every sync
1. **Every number traces to a real artifact** (D-003). Never invent a stat to fill a card.
2. **Positioning is fixed** (D-001): AI full stack leads, 3D supports. A new 3D
   project does not get promoted above the code work.
3. **Ask before dropping a featured card.** Adding is a judgment call; removing
   something Luqman chose is his decision.
4. **Verify by rendering** before pushing — see the pattern above.
5. Commit and `git push`, then **`./tools/deploy.sh`** — only the deploy script publishes.

### Quick sync
```bash
cd ~/Desktop/project/web/portfolio
# edit index.html, then:
CHROME="/Applications/Google Chrome.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome"
"$CHROME" --headless --disable-gpu --virtual-time-budget=8000 \
  --window-size=1440,1400 --screenshot=/tmp/shot.png \
  --enable-logging=stderr --v=0 file://$PWD/index.html 2>&1 | grep -i CONSOLE
git add -A && git commit -m "update <project> card" && git push
```

## Adding a project to the gallery

The gallery is static markup in `index.html` (filtering is the only JavaScript), so a
new project means one new card. Copy the nearest existing card and change it.

```html
<a class="work rv" data-cat="code" href="REPO_URL" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
  <div class="shot"><img src="img/SLUG.jpg" alt="WHAT THE IMAGE SHOWS"
       loading="lazy" width="880" height="660"></div>
  <div class="work-body">
    <div class="work-head"><h3>NAME</h3><span class="cat cat-code">Coding</span></div>
    <p>WHAT IT IS AND WHAT WAS HARD ABOUT IT.</p>
    <div class="chips"><span class="chip">TECH</span></div>
    <span class="foot">View repository <span class="arrow">→</span></span>
  </div>
</a>
```

- `data-cat` and the `.cat-*` class must agree: `code`/`cat-code`, `game`/`cat-game`,
  `3d`/`cat-3d`, `hw`/`cat-hw`.
- Add `wide` to the class list to make it span two columns. Use it sparingly — two or
  three per page.
- **Update the counts** in the `.filters` buttons and the "Projects on this page" stat.
- Use `<div>` instead of `<a>` when there is nothing to link to.

### Getting an image
In order of preference:
1. **Render the thing itself** headless — this is how `print-quote`, `peta-lot-tanah`
   and `tuition-lms` were done. Add `--enable-unsafe-swiftshader --use-gl=angle
   --use-angle=swiftshader` whenever the page uses WebGL, or the canvas comes out blank.
2. **An existing screenshot** in the project repo (`docs/`, `press/`, `dev/ref/screens/`).
3. **The Behance cover** — see `07-references.md`.
4. **A typographic tile** (`.shot.tile`) when there is genuinely no image.
   Never fake a screenshot.

Then normalise it:
```bash
sips -Z 880 shot.png                                     # longest side 880px
sips -s format jpeg -s formatOptions 82 shot.png --out img/slug.jpg
```
`sips -c` crops from the **centre**, not the top — to capture just the top of a page,
re-render at a shorter `--window-size` instead of cropping.

## Rendering image cards (Behance, or anywhere text must ship as an image)

Behance has no usable write API and its project body is image modules, not text — so a
description can be delivered as a rendered card instead. The generator is
`~/.claude/knowledge/projects/portfolio/assets/make_cards.py` (copy kept alongside the
scratch original); output goes to `~/Desktop/project/web/portfolio/behance-cards/`, which is
git-ignored because it is an upload deliverable, not part of the site.

**Design of the series:** 1400px wide (Behance's display width), cool near-black
`#101319`, periwinkle accent `#93a6ff` tying back to the portfolio's lilac family,
Archivo 800 titles / Source Serif 4 body / JetBrains Mono labels. Each card is a
**composite**: the project's own hero image on top, then eyebrow, title, full
description, Role, Tools and the signature block — so one upload replaces a whole
project post.

**Pick the hero by looking, not by position.** The first module is often a title slide
or a raw screenshot of SketchUp/Blender with every panel showing — six of twelve were,
on 2026-09-13. Download every module, lay them out as a contact sheet (an HTML grid
screenshotted with Chrome does it in one look), then choose. A software screenshot can
be rescued by cropping the viewport out of the chrome — but **measure the crop on the
exact image you are using**, since module layouts differ; a box measured on a sibling
screenshot left a strip of UI on both edges. When a project genuinely has no artwork,
ship the card with no hero rather than a title card.

**Where the hero images come from.** Behance serves project images at
`mir-s3-cdn-cf.behance.net/project_modules/<bucket>/<hash>.<ext>`; the `1400` and
`fs` buckets are full quality, while the profile grid only exposes `max_808` covers.
Pull the module URLs out of a saved project page in document order — the first is
usually the intended hero. **Exception:** the handheld console's four modules are all
Tinkercad screenshots; its only photograph of the finished device is the project
*cover*, whose largest form is `projects/original/<hash>` at 810x1080 (the other size
buckets 302-redirect). Sources are archived in `assets/hero/`.

**Never upscale the hero.** An image at least 1400 wide goes full-bleed; anything
narrower sits centred at its native size on the dark ground. Stretching an 810px photo
to 1400 is visibly soft, and the letterbox reads as deliberate on a dark card.

**Give the hero explicit `width`/`height` attributes** so page height is deterministic
before the image loads — otherwise the measurement below races image decoding.

**The part worth remembering — measure, don't guess the height.** A fixed canvas height
leaves a dead gap under short text and clips long text. Render twice:

```python
# pass 1 - let the page report its own height once webfonts have settled
#   in the page:  document.fonts.ready.then(() => document.title = "H"+
#                   Math.ceil(document.body.getBoundingClientRect().height)+"H")
chrome --headless --virtual-time-budget=12000 --window-size=1400,400 --dump-dom file://card.html
#   then parse <title>H(\d+)H</title>
# pass 2 - shoot at exactly that height
chrome --headless --hide-scrollbars --force-device-scale-factor=1 \
       --virtual-time-budget=12000 --window-size=1400,<measured> \
       --screenshot=out.png file://card.html
```

`--force-device-scale-factor=1` keeps the pixel size honest; `document.fonts.ready` is
required or the height is measured against the fallback font and comes out short.

## Testing interactive JavaScript headlessly

`node --check` only catches syntax. For anything with a state machine — the Line Call
game, the easter egg — drive it and assert on the result.

**`requestAnimationFrame` does not run under `--dump-dom`** (0 frames in 1.5s; 1 under
`--headless=new`), even though `setTimeout` advances fine with `--virtual-time-budget`.
Shim it in the harness, before the code under test starts its loop:

```js
window.requestAnimationFrame = function(fn){
  return setTimeout(function(){ fn(performance.now()); }, 16);
};
window.cancelAnimationFrame = clearTimeout;
```

Only frame pacing is faked — the real state machine, maths and scoring all run. Write
the harness as a copy of `index.html` with a driver appended before `</body>`, have it
push findings into an array and end with `document.title = "@@" + log.join(" | ") + "@@"`,
then read it back with `--dump-dom | grep -o '@@.*@@'`. Delete the copy afterwards.

**`IntersectionObserver` callbacks do not re-fire on scroll in that mode**, so anything
gated on visibility cannot be exercised headlessly — test those branches by calling the
code path directly, and check them by hand in a real browser.

For visual checks use `--screenshot`, which *does* drive rAF.

## Gotchas / traps
| Problem | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Page silently loses confetti + easter egg on some machines | `new THREE.WebGLRenderer()` *throws* without WebGL, and the uncaught error aborts the rest of the enclosing IIFE | Wrap construction in `try/catch`, remove the canvas, `return` |
| `TypeError` on load | Assigning a property to a number primitive is a throw under `"use strict"` | — |
| Falling game piece detached from the rope once the tower scrolled | Piece spawned in screen coordinates but drawn with the camera offset applied | Spawn at `p0.y - camY` |
| Mobile "looks broken" in headless screenshots | Viewport clamped to 500px, image cropped to the requested width | Use the iframe method above |
| Images broken when testing a copy of the page | A copy saved outside the project folder cannot resolve relative `img/` paths | Write the test copy into the project folder, then delete it |
| Card title and subtitle run together on a tile | `.tname`/`.tsub` are `<span>`, which is inline | They are set `display:block` — keep it that way |
| WebGL canvas blank in a screenshot | Headless Chrome has no GPU by default | `--enable-unsafe-swiftshader --use-gl=angle --use-angle=swiftshader` |
| Rendered card has a dead gap or clipped text | The canvas height was fixed instead of measured | Two-pass render — see "Rendering image cards" above |
| A canvas game "renders but does nothing" in a headless test | rAF never fires under `--dump-dom` | Shim rAF onto setTimeout in the harness |
| Verdict marker invisible in a game | It was drawn before the sprite that sits on the same spot | Draw the thing the player is looking for last |

## The project records live in the portfolio (2026-09-18)

Luqman asked for the documentation to be combined into the portfolio. It now is,
and this is the arrangement:

- Each project still generates its own record into its own repo at
  `docs/index.html` (`python3 tools/docs/build_docs.py`, or `docs-build` for the
  non-game projects).
- `docs-site build` gathers every one of those into
  `~/Desktop/project/web/portfolio/records/`, one folder per project plus an
  index. That folder is part of the portfolio, so `tools/deploy.sh` publishes
  the records with it at `https://luqmanhakeem.pages.dev/records/` — behind
  the sign-in (see "The records are private" below).
- The portfolio's own record stays at `docs/index.html` in that repo, and is
  copied into `records/portfolio/` like any other. The records site is written
  to `records/` and not `docs/` precisely so it cannot overwrite that, or be
  picked up by the scanner as a record of its own.
- The eleven **Read the record** links on the project cards point at
  `records/<project>/index.html` - relative, so they work both from a file on
  disk and from the published site.

This replaced Netlify, which was dropped the day before when a deploy was
refused for want of account credit. Nothing costs anything now: the portfolio
was already being published, and the records ride along with it.

**The records are private (since 2026-09-23).** They contain the full session
logs, decisions and methods. They were public until Luqman asked for them to be
reachable only through a login — see D-005 and the section below.

**Size.** The records are about 25 MB in total, almost all of it screenshots
embedded as JPEG data URIs, and every rebuild rewrites the files. The repo will
grow steadily with each push. Worth watching, and worth pruning galleries if it
ever becomes a problem.

## The body is a narrower column than the header and footer (2026-09-18)

At Luqman's asking: *"i dont want the portfolio body to fill the space, i want it
only to be in the middle, let there be a gap on the left and right. the header
and footer will be the same."*

So `.wrap` - which is only ever `<main>` - is 960px rather than 1180px, and the
contact block breaks back out with `margin: 56px -130px 28px` to regain the top
bar's 1180. Both collapse on narrow screens.

Two traps, both of which cost time:

- **A `margin` shorthand later in the sheet resets the sides.** There is a second
  `.foot-wrap` rule further down; setting `margin-inline` higher up did nothing
  at all until the break-out was moved into the rule that actually wins.
- **Do not centre the break-out with `margin-left:50%; transform:translateX(-50%)`.**
  It measures the 50% against the *narrow* parent, and the block walks off the
  left of the page.

## The records wear the portfolio's palette (2026-09-18)

Asked for the day they were combined into the portfolio. Since they are
published as part of it, they now use its colours rather than the blue-grey
sports palette every record inherited from referee-for-fun:

| role | portfolio variable | value |
|---|---|---|
| page | `--paper` | `#FAF7F2` |
| cards | — | `#FFFFFF` |
| text | `--ink` | `#2B2622` |
| quiet text | `--ink-soft` | `#6B6259` |
| rules and borders | `--line` | `#E7E1D8` (the solid equivalent of the portfolio's 10% ink) |
| links and anything live | `--sky-ink` / `--sky` | `#2F6BB0` / `#D6E6FA` |
| labels and chips | `--butter` / `--butter-ink` | `#FFEEC9` / `#A97B12` |
| done, passing, good | `--mint-ink` | `#2F7A5C` |

Two things worth knowing about how this was applied:

- **The templates come in two generations.** Five of them keep the palette in
  `LIGHT` and `DARK` constants; direct-hit and referee-for-fun still write it
  straight into the `:root` rules, and use different variable names for the same
  roles (`--court` where the others say `--accent`, `--caption` for `--flag`).
  The colours were remapped **by role**, not by name, so both shapes land on the
  same palette.
- **Dark mode is gone, deliberately.** The portfolio is light-only, so a record
  that flipped to a dark theme on a machine set to dark would stop matching it
  exactly when it mattered. The dark blocks now resolve to the same light
  values, and `color-scheme` is `light` throughout - without that last part the
  scrollbars and form controls stay dark on a pale page.

Only the colours were changed. The records still use their own typeface and
their uppercase headings; matching those to the portfolio's Plus Jakarta Sans
would unify them further and has not been done.

## The records sign-in and the Cloudflare deploy (2026-09-23)

- **Where the lock is.** `functions/records/_middleware.js` runs on Cloudflare
  before any file under `/records/` is sent. No valid session cookie → a 302 to
  `/?signin=/records/...`, which opens the sign-in box on the portfolio and
  returns the visitor to the record afterwards. The box in `index.html` is only
  the entrance; hiding it would not unlock anything.
- **The session** is an HttpOnly cookie `records_session` = `<expiry>.<HMAC>`,
  valid 30 days, signed with `SESSION_SECRET`. `functions/_auth.js` holds the
  helpers; `/api/login`, `/api/logout`, `/api/session` are the endpoints.
- **The credentials** are Cloudflare Pages secrets on the `luqmanhakeem`
  project — `RECORDS_USER`, `RECORDS_PASSWORD`, `SESSION_SECRET` — with copies
  in `~/.claude/.env` as `PORTFOLIO_RECORDS_USER`, `PORTFOLIO_RECORDS_PASSWORD`,
  `PORTFOLIO_SESSION_SECRET`. Change one with
  `printf '%s' NEW | npx wrangler pages secret put RECORDS_PASSWORD --project-name luqmanhakeem`
  (with `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` set), then redeploy. Changing `SESSION_SECRET`
  signs everyone out.
- **Every record link is gated**, not just the nav button: a click on any
  `a[href^="records/"]` asks `/api/session` first. Opened from disk
  (`file://`) there is no server, so the script steps aside.
- **The deploy** stages a copy without `.git`, `functions/`, `tools/`, `docs/`,
  `behance-cards/` and dotfiles, then runs `wrangler pages deploy` *from the repo
  root* — wrangler finds `functions/` in the working directory, not in the
  staged folder.
- **Preview URLs are locked too.** Every deploy gets a `<hash>.luqmanhakeem.pages.dev`
  address; the middleware runs there as well (checked: 302).

